Z||000000||sqoach||11-20-2000||04:44 PM||||well i posted my info on my old topic, but i'll make another just in case you hate lookin at that old one.  i took a compression check on my car and i think i have bad news.  i'll give you the numbers for the tester. remember i have a 4 cyl.(starting from the left)<P>1-70; 2-50; 3-30; 4-45<P>so all you experts, tell me what's up and what these numbers mean.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||11
Z||000001||Seanh||11-20-2000||04:51 PM||||Well, it could be any number of things. But, a good engine should have all above 100. It could be worn out rings, cracked block or heads, head gasket, bad valves, things like that. I an not a 4cyl owner so I cant really be of much more specific help.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||11-20-2000||04:58 PM||||i'm gonna do a recheck on my numbers there...i think i mighta done somethin wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-20-2000||04:58 PM||||Aha!  I see the problem.  Think the cylinders are ordinarily numbered from right to left.  So, what you really have is:<P>4: 45psi;  3: 30psi;  2: 50psi;  1: 70psi<P>That better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>Actually, first thing I'd do, is verify that your compression tester / technique are good.  For example, did you prop the throttle open when you did the test?  Does the tester leak?  Are you sure you screwed the gage into the spark plug hole properly so it doesn't leak?  Might try the gage on another (known good) engine, and see what happens. <P>If those are accurate readings, it doesn't look good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||11-20-2000||05:28 PM||||ok i rechecked it and i guess i didn't turn it over enough times.  here's the correct numbers:<BR>(and in the correct order)<P>4-90; 3-90; 2-120; 1-120<P>those are approximate, maybe off 1 or 2<P>i'm going to work right now, so i'll be back later.  about 3 or 4 hours from now.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||11-20-2000||05:37 PM||||My guess is a blown head gasket.  Right between #3 & #4.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-20-2000||07:08 PM||||Could also be weak or a broken head bolt. GM had a very big batch of crappy bolts in the 4 cyls. The exhaust side ones are prone to break. They are mentioned in both the fire recall and a seperate TSB for 84-87 4 cyl.<P>before worring about a full rebuild you might consider just replacing the head gasket and bolts. (Assuming the head and/or block hasn't been damaged by having a bad gasket in ther.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||11-20-2000||07:15 PM||||I think you should redo the test and squirt some oil in the cyl first. That will tell you the condition of the rings.<P>I agree with the guys above that it looks like a blown head guasket or bolts BUT it's an easy test and it could be rings.||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||11-20-2000||09:19 PM||||well at least evry1 is agreeing on the same thing.  the guy that i borrowed the tester from also said it might be those.  but, is this what's causing my car to overheat???  and how much is it gonna cost?<P>i'm gonna check all this stuff tomorrow.<P>thanks guys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||11-21-2000||07:42 PM||||the head bolts or head gasket leaking could also cause over heat. <P>They can also cause a "false" overheat. The engine leaks compression into the cooling system which then acts like it's hot and blows coolant all over. If this is happening it usually show up within a couple minuts of starting the car.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||11-21-2000||08:04 PM||||i guess i forgot to mention that my car got so hot one time, that it shut off.  i was taking a little trip a few miles out of town and i noticed dramatic decrease in power after driving for a few minutes.  when i would push on the gas, it would make a strange noise, kind of like a diesel truck.  it then just finally turned off.  i waited for it to cool off, then drove it home and barely made it.  when i take really short trips, like going to work or to class, it does ok.  but when i drive for more than about 10 minutes at a time, it overheats, and antifreeze runs over.<P>i think my catalytic converter is bad too, but i don't know if that would cause an overheat or not.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||1
Z||000011||sqoach||11-21-2000||09:26 PM||||just gettin my topic back to the top  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||11-21-2000||11:04 PM||||Do you notice any smoke from the tailpipes, more then normal? It could be that coolant is leaking into the chambers causing your lack of power, and maybe you lost engough coolant the water pump air-locked and the car overheated. If it was bad enought to shut the car off, you probally have a bad head gasket at the very least. I would tend to think cracked head or maybe block. Check to make sure your fan is turning on when the coolant reaches normal temp. Or, turn on the A/C and see if the fan comes on. ||||reg||1
